-- block-layoffs-second-round-2026-cash-app-square-20261106 (1000 words; primary keyword in title, first paragraph, subheading)
INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'block-layoffs-second-round-2026-cash-app-square-20261106', 'global', 'United States',
'Block Layoffs Hit Cash App and Square Teams in Second Round of 2026',
'',
'Block layoffs have begun for a second time in 2026, hitting the teams behind Cash App and Square. The fintech firm has not disclosed how many staff are affected, while US employees are being offered at least 60 days of pay plus severance.',
'',
$$<p>Block layoffs have begun for a second time in 2026, with Jack Dorsey's fintech company cutting jobs across the teams that support Cash App and Square. Staff were told their roles were being eliminated on Wednesday 4 November 2026, according to a Business Insider report, and the company has not disclosed how many employees are affected. The cuts come one day after Block reported third-quarter results and raised its profit forecast for the year.</p>
<p>The reductions hit Customer Operations, Risk Operations and Learning and Content Operations, the teams handling customer support, risk checks and training content for Cash App and Square. According to Business Insider, those teams will reduce the number of roles, change some responsibilities and shift some reporting lines. Staff in the United States, Canada, the United Kingdom and Australia are affected, although the company has not said how the reductions are distributed across those countries.</p>
<h2>Block layoffs follow a much larger cut in February</h2>
<p>The November round is Block's second major workforce reduction of 2026. In February, the company eliminated around 4,000 jobs, more than 40 per cent of a workforce of about 10,000 employees. At the time, Dorsey said the February cuts were partly driven by advances in how much work AI could take on, calling them "intelligence tools" that let smaller teams do more.</p>
<p>The language around the latest cuts is different. In the email to staff, Block referred to "using better tools to handle routine tasks" and "refocusing our teams' time on complex cases and decisions". The communication did not mention artificial intelligence. Either way, the direction is the same: fewer people on routine work, with the rest focused on harder cases.</p>
<p>Block has not explained what drove this round. The timing is striking because the company is not in financial difficulty: it reported third-quarter results on Tuesday 3 November 2026 and raised its 2026 gross profit forecast to US$12.63 billion, up from US$12.51 billion. The results were better than expected, with growth at both Square and Cash App. A company cutting jobs the day after raising its profit forecast is cutting for efficiency and shape, not for survival.</p>
<h2>What affected Block staff are being offered</h2>
<p>US-based employees affected by the latest Block layoffs are being offered a separation package, according to an internal FAQ document seen by Business Insider. The package includes at least 60 days of pay and a minimum of seven weeks of base salary as severance, with an additional week of base pay for every year an employee worked at Block. Staff who sign a separation agreement get a further 30 days of pay, bringing the total to 90 days, plus a one-time US$5,000 transition payment. Eligible employees and their dependants also receive three months of COBRA health cover.</p>
<p>The terms differ from February, when affected staff received 20 weeks of salary plus an additional week for each year at the company. The minimum severance and 90 days of pay without working are roughly equivalent to the 20 weeks offered in February, before the additional tenure-based pay. Both rounds included the US$5,000 transition payment. The figures in the FAQ describe the US package; employees elsewhere face terms based on their location.</p>
<p>A Block spokesperson declined to comment. The company told some workers on Wednesday that their roles were being eliminated, and some Square and Cash App staff reported job losses on social media. The scale was not immediately clear, and Block has not detailed the new terms publicly.</p>
<h2>Block layoffs are part of a wider tech pattern</h2>
<p>Block is not the only technology firm making reductions this week. <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/canva-layoffs-1271-jobs-20261105">Canva said it would let go of 22 per cent of its workforce</a> as part of a reorganisation, and <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/ai-layoffs-reversed-companies-rehire-roles-20261105">other firms that cut roles for AI reasons have already had to rehire some of them</a>. The pattern is consistent: companies are using AI and better tools to automate routine work, then reshaping the teams that remain around complex decisions, risk judgement and customer relationships that software cannot handle alone.</p>
<p>For workers in customer operations and risk roles, the message is uncomfortable but clear. Routine case handling is the work most exposed to automation, and the teams doing it are the ones being reduced. The skills that survive are judgement under uncertainty, handling complex cases, and the product and risk knowledge that lets a worker decide when the automated answer is wrong.</p>
<p>The February cuts were presented as a strategy to run Block with smaller teams and AI tools, aiming for about US$800 million to US$900 million in yearly savings. The November round continues that strategy, and it shows that a single round of cuts does not finish the job: once a company decides smaller teams plus better tools is the model, further rounds follow as the tools improve.</p>
<h2>What to do next</h2>
<p>If you work at Block, read the internal FAQ document and your separation terms in full before signing anything, and note any deadline for signing the separation agreement, since the extra 30 days of pay depends on it. Keep copies of the email, the FAQ and any written communication about your role, and confirm your eligibility for the US$5,000 transition payment and three months of COBRA cover.</p>
<p>If you are a jobseeker in fintech, the Block layoffs do not mean fintech hiring has stopped. Payment companies and banks still hire for risk, compliance, fraud prevention and customer experience roles, but the roles now ask for more: data skills, an understanding of automation tools and the judgement to handle cases the tools escalate. Update your CV with the complex cases you handled and the tools you used, not just the volume of cases you closed.</p>
<p>Check Block's careers page and those of other fintech employers directly, and never pay anyone who promises a job: genuine employers never charge to apply. The honest picture is that Block is reshaping itself around smaller teams and better tools, and workers who build the skills that fit that shape will have the best options.</p>
<table><tr><td>Company</td><td>Block, fintech firm behind Cash App and Square</td></tr><tr><td>Latest cuts began</td><td>Wednesday 4 November 2026</td></tr><tr><td>Teams affected</td><td>Customer Operations, Risk Operations, Learning and Content Operations</td></tr><tr><td>Number of roles cut</td><td>Not disclosed by the company</td></tr><tr><td>US severance</td><td>At least 60 days of pay plus a minimum of seven weeks of base salary</td></tr><tr><td>Extra for signing separation agreement</td><td>30 more days of pay, taking the total to 90 days</td></tr><tr><td>Transition payment</td><td>One-time US$5,000</td></tr><tr><td>Health cover</td><td>Three months of COBRA for eligible employees and dependants</td></tr><tr><td>February 2026 cuts</td><td>Around 4,000 jobs, more than 40 per cent of the workforce</td></tr><tr><td>2026 gross profit forecast</td><td>Raised to US$12.63 billion from US$12.51 billion on 3 November 2026</td></tr></table>
<p><small>Sources: Moneycontrol at https://www.moneycontrol.com/technology/jack-dorsey-s-block-announces-fresh-layoffs-second-round-of-job-cuts-in-2026-report-article-14072384.html/amp?utm_campaign=fullarticle&amp;utm_medium=referral&amp;utm_source=inshorts on the second round of cuts, the affected teams and the severance terms; Fast Company at https://www.fastcompany.com/91636771/jack-dorseys-block-is-cutting-jobs-again-heres-what-makes-these-layoffs-different?utm_source=linkedin&amp;utm_medium=social&amp;trk=organization_guest_main-feed-card-text on the timing, the February cuts and the third-quarter results.</small></p>
$$,
'',
ARRAY['https://www.moneycontrol.com/technology/jack-dorsey-s-block-announces-fresh-layoffs-second-round-of-job-cuts-in-2026-report-article-14072384.html/amp?utm_campaign=fullarticle&utm_medium=referral&utm_source=inshorts','https://www.fastcompany.com/91636771/jack-dorseys-block-is-cutting-jobs-again-heres-what-makes-these-layoffs-different?utm_source=linkedin&utm_medium=social&trk=organization_guest_main-feed-card-text'],
NOW(), NOW(), NOW())
ON CONFLICT ("slug") DO NOTHING;

-- upsc-mains-result-2026-2526-qualify-interview-20261106 (966 words; primary keyword in title, first paragraph, subheading)
INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'upsc-mains-result-2026-2526-qualify-interview-20261106', 'global', 'India',
'UPSC Mains Result 2026 Declared: 2,526 Candidates Qualify for Interview',
'',
'The UPSC Mains result 2026 has been declared, with 2,526 candidates qualifying for the Personality Test. The written result was published on 5 November 2026, and shortlisted candidates must update their online forms between 11 and 25 November 2026.',
'',
$$<p>The UPSC Mains result 2026 has been declared, with 2,526 candidates qualifying for the Personality Test, the final stage of the Civil Services Examination. The Union Public Service Commission published the written result on 5 November 2026, ending weeks of waiting for thousands of aspirants across India. The shortlisted candidates will now compete for an estimated 933 posts across the Indian Administrative Service, the Indian Foreign Service, the Indian Police Service and other Central Services.</p>
<p>The result is a roll-number-wise and name-wise PDF on the commission's official website, upsc.gov.in. Candidates can check their status by opening the PDF and searching for their roll number; no login is needed. The commission has said the candidature of shortlisted candidates remains provisional and subject to verification of eligibility conditions and supporting documents.</p>
<h2>UPSC Mains result 2026: the numbers behind the shortlist</h2>
<p>The Civil Services (Main) Examination 2026 was held from 21 August to 30 August 2026. A total of 13,343 candidates had qualified for the written examination after clearing the Civil Services Preliminary Examination earlier in the year, and appeared for the Mains. Of those, 2,526 have now been shortlisted for the Personality Test, which is about 18.9 per cent of the candidates who sat the written papers.</p>
<p>The Personality Test carries 275 marks, and the final ranking rests on the written papers counted for merit plus the interview: the written examination carried 1,750 marks, so the final merit total is 2,025. The commission has not announced the interview schedule; the dates will be published on the UPSC website in due course. The Personality Tests will be held at the commission's office at Dholpur House, Shahjahan Road, New Delhi, 110069.</p>
<p>The published list is arranged by roll number, and its serial numbers are not All India Ranks. Qualifying for the interview does not mean final selection: the interview is a genuine test, and the final merit list will rank candidates on combined written and interview marks. The commission has not announced individual Mains marks or cut-off marks. Marksheets will be uploaded on the commission's website within 15 days of the declaration of the final result, after the Personality Tests.</p>
<h2>What shortlisted candidates must do next</h2>
<p>Candidates shortlisted for the Personality Test are required to fill in or update the requisite details in their online application forms. The facility will be available on the UPSC online portal, upsconline.nic.in, from 11 November to 25 November 2026, until 6:00 PM. Candidates who miss this window risk missing the interview call, so the form should be completed well before the closing time rather than left to the last day, when the portal can be slow.</p>
<p>Individual e-summon letters for the Personality Test will be uploaded on the commission's official website. Candidates should check upsc.gov.in regularly for the interview schedule and their e-summon letter, and should not rely on social media posts or forwarded messages for dates. The commission will publish the schedule itself, and any date circulating before that announcement is not official.</p>
<p>Preparation for the interview should start now, not when the schedule appears. The Personality Test is a conversation with a board, not another written paper, and it tests judgement, awareness of current affairs and the ability to explain a point of view clearly. Candidates should revise their detailed application form, since the board will ask about the background, hobbies and choices recorded there. <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/ai-jobs-india-hiring-october-naukri-jobspeak-20261103">India's job market is also shifting towards AI skills</a>, and an awareness of how technology is changing governance is useful material for the interview room.</p>
<h2>UPSC Mains result 2026: what it means for those who did not qualify</h2>
<p>For the roughly 10,800 candidates who sat the Mains but did not make the shortlist, the result is a hard disappointment after months of preparation. The competition is severe by design: about one in five Mains candidates reaches the interview, and only about 933 posts are available. Many strong candidates miss out in a given year, and a large share of those who eventually succeed do so on a later attempt.</p>
<p>The practical next step is to wait for the marksheets, which the commission will upload within 15 days of the final result, and use them to find where marks were lost. Candidates who were close should look at answer writing and optional subject preparation; candidates who were far off should consider a different strategy. <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/ai-jobs-india-gcc-hiring-quess-report-20261105">Hiring for AI and technology roles in India and the Gulf is growing</a>, and for some candidates a parallel career path in the private sector is worth building alongside the next attempt.</p>
<p>The result also matters beyond the candidates. The Civil Services Examination is India's most competitive recruitment process, and the 2,526 now shortlisted are the group from which the next batch of district magistrates, diplomats, police officers and central service officers will come. Their interviews will run over the coming months, and the final result will follow.</p>
<h2>Check only the official website</h2>
<p>Candidates should check the result only on upsc.gov.in and complete the form only on upsconline.nic.in. The result PDF is free, and the commission never asks for money to declare a result or issue an e-summon letter. Any message, call or website asking for payment in exchange for a place on the list, an interview date or a posting is a scam. Candidates should also be careful with fake PDFs and edited screenshots on messaging apps: the only list that counts is the one on the commission's own website.</p>
<p>If your roll number is on the list, congratulations, and start preparing for the interview now. If it is not, take the time to recover, read the marksheet when it appears, and decide the next step with a clear head. The examination rewards persistence, and the 2,526 names on this list include many who did not succeed on their first attempt.</p>
<table><tr><td>Result declared</td><td>5 November 2026</td></tr><tr><td>Candidates qualified for interview</td><td>2,526</td></tr><tr><td>Candidates who sat the Mains</td><td>13,343</td></tr><tr><td>Estimated vacancies</td><td>About 933</td></tr><tr><td>Next stage</td><td>Personality Test (interview), 275 marks</td></tr><tr><td>Final merit total</td><td>2,025 marks (1,750 written plus 275 interview)</td></tr><tr><td>Form update window</td><td>11 to 25 November 2026, till 6:00 PM, on upsconline.nic.in</td></tr><tr><td>Interview venue</td><td>Dholpur House, Shahjahan Road, New Delhi, 110069</td></tr><tr><td>Interview schedule</td><td>Not yet announced</td></tr><tr><td>Official website</td><td>upsc.gov.in</td></tr></table>
<p><small>Sources: Education Times at https://www.educationtimes.com/news/upsc-cse-mains-result-2026-declared-2526-candidates-shortlisted-for-personality-test/articleshow/135596359.cms on the result, the 2,526 shortlisted candidates and the form window; The Economic Times at https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/new-updates/upsc-mains-result-2026-who-has-qualified-for-interview-round-check-list-of-2526-candidates-who-cleared-written-exam-and-download-the-pdf-here/articleshow/135580937.cms on the result date, the Mains dates and the interview venue.</small></p>
$$,
'',
ARRAY['https://www.educationtimes.com/news/upsc-cse-mains-result-2026-declared-2526-candidates-shortlisted-for-personality-test/articleshow/135596359.cms','https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/new-updates/upsc-mains-result-2026-who-has-qualified-for-interview-round-check-list-of-2526-candidates-who-cleared-written-exam-and-download-the-pdf-here/articleshow/135580937.cms'],
NOW(), NOW(), NOW())
ON CONFLICT ("slug") DO NOTHING;
